// heartsim tutorial: murmurs and where to listen. Original text by the heartsim authors,
// MIT OR Apache-2.0 (see ASSETS-LICENSES.md). Format: see crates/heartsim-core/src/tutorial.rs.
// The murmurs are the model of brief B27; every clinical statement below carries its source in the
// references list.
Tutorial(
    format_version: 1,
    id: "12_murmurs",
    title: "Murmurs and where to listen",
    summary: "Systolic against diastolic murmurs, the ejection murmur of aortic stenosis against the innocent murmur and against a regurgitant one, aortic regurgitation, mitral stenosis with its snap and rumble, and the five listening sites.",
    references: [
        "Alpert JS. In: Walker HK, Hall WD, Hurst JW (eds). Clinical Methods, 3rd ed. Butterworths 1990, ch. 26. ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK345.",
        "Crawley IS. In: Clinical Methods, 3rd ed. Butterworths 1990, ch. 27. ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK346.",
        "Felner JM. The first heart sound. In: Clinical Methods, 3rd ed. Butterworths 1990, ch. 22. ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK333.",
        "MSD Manual Professional Version: Cardiac auscultation; Heart murmurs. msdmanuals.com/professional/cardiovascular-disorders/approach-to-the-cardiac-patient/cardiac-auscultation.",
        "Finley JP, Caissie R, Nicol P, Hoyt B. International trends in the teaching of auscultation and the diagnostic reliability of murmurs. Med Educ 1998;32:409-414. doi:10.1046/j.1365-2923.1998.00210.x.",
        "Barrett MJ, Lacey CS, Sekara AE, Linden EA, Gracely EJ. Mastering cardiac murmurs: the power of repetition. Chest 2004;126:470-475. doi:10.1378/chest.126.2.470.",
    ],
    pages: [
        Page(
            title: "Systolic or diastolic?",
            markdown: r#"
A **murmur** is the sound of turbulent flow. The first question about any murmur is **where it lies
in the cycle**: between S1 and S2 it is **systolic**, between S2 and the next S1 it is
**diastolic** (MSD Manual). Find S1 first (with the QRS, followed by the shorter pause), then place
the murmur.

Mitral regurgitation is loaded, at the apex. On the lane the murmur fills the space **between S1 and
S2**. If you have no sound, read the lane: the murmur is the band between the labels.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("mitral_regurgitation")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(HeartSoundAudio(true)),
                Ui(HeartSoundLane(true)),
                Ui(Listen(site: Mitral, chest: Diaphragm)),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "This murmur lies between S1 and S2. It is:",
                choices: ["Systolic", "Diastolic", "Continuous", "Presystolic"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "Between S1 and S2 is systole. This one starts with S1 and stays level up to S2: a holosystolic, regurgitant murmur.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "The ejection murmur of aortic stenosis",
            markdown: r#"
Blood forced through a narrow aortic valve makes an **ejection** murmur: it starts a little after
S1, once the valve opens, **rises and falls** (crescendo-decrescendo, "diamond") and ends before
A2. The more severe the stenosis, the **later the peak** and the **softer A2** (Alpert, ch. 26). It
is loudest at the **aortic area**, the 2nd intercostal space at the right sternal border.

Severe aortic stenosis is loaded, at the aortic area.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("aortic_stenosis_severe")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(HeartSoundAudio(true)),
                Ui(HeartSoundLane(true)),
                Ui(Listen(site: Aortic, chest: Diaphragm)),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "The shape of an ejection murmur is:",
                choices: [
                    "Crescendo-decrescendo, with a gap after S1",
                    "Level from S1 to S2",
                    "Decrescendo from S2",
                    "A low rumble in mid-diastole",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "Ejection starts once the valve opens, after S1, and the flow rises and falls: the diamond shape. A level murmur from S1 is regurgitant.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Innocent or not?",
            markdown: r#"
Learners call too many innocent murmurs abnormal (Finley et al. 1998). An **innocent murmur** is
**short, soft** and often **musical**, lies in early to mid systole, and comes with a **normal S2**
and nothing else: no click, no extra sound, nothing in diastole (MSD Manual).

The innocent murmur of a healthy heart is loaded, at the lower left sternal border. Compare it with
the previous page; the **Sound Trainer** has the two as a contrast pair you can switch between with
one key.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("innocent_murmur")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(HeartSoundAudio(true)),
                Ui(HeartSoundLane(true)),
                Ui(Listen(site: Tricuspid, chest: Diaphragm)),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "Which finding goes with an innocent murmur?",
                choices: [
                    "A short, soft systolic murmur with a normal S2",
                    "A diastolic murmur",
                    "An ejection click and a single S2",
                    "A murmur that lasts through S2",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "Innocent murmurs are short, soft and systolic, with normal heart sounds. Anything diastolic, a click, or an abnormal S2 points elsewhere.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "A diastolic murmur from S2: aortic regurgitation",
            markdown: r#"
In **aortic regurgitation** blood leaks back from the aorta as soon as the valve closes, so the
murmur **starts at A2** and **fades away** (decrescendo) through diastole. It is high-pitched and
blowing, heard best along the left sternal border at **Erb's point** (3rd intercostal space) with
the diaphragm (Crawley, ch. 27; MSD Manual).
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("aortic_regurgitation")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(HeartSoundAudio(true)),
                Ui(HeartSoundLane(true)),
                Ui(Listen(site: Erb, chest: Diaphragm)),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "The murmur of aortic regurgitation:",
                choices: [
                    "Starts at S2 and fades through diastole",
                    "Starts after S1 and rises and falls",
                    "Is a low rumble just before S1",
                    "Lies only in late systole",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "Regurgitation begins the moment the aortic valve closes, at A2, and the backflow falls off through diastole: an early diastolic decrescendo.",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Mitral stenosis: a snap and a rumble",
            markdown: r#"
A stiff mitral valve gives three findings: a **loud S1**, an **opening snap** shortly after S2 as
the valve opens, and a **low rumble** in mid-diastole that grows louder again **just before S1** in
sinus rhythm, when the atrium contracts (Felner, ch. 22; Crawley, ch. 27). The rumble is low, so
use the **bell** at the apex.

Mitral stenosis in sinus rhythm is loaded. On the lane, find **OS** after S2.
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("mitral_stenosis_sinus")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(HeartSoundAudio(true)),
                Ui(HeartSoundLane(true)),
                Ui(Listen(site: Mitral, chest: Bell)),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "Which chest piece brings out the rumble of mitral stenosis?",
                choices: ["The bell", "The diaphragm", "Either, it makes no difference", "Neither: it is only seen on the ECG"],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "The rumble is a low-frequency sound, and the bell passes low frequencies; the diaphragm favours the high ones (the snap, S2, regurgitant murmurs).",
            )),
        ),
        Page(
            title: "Where to listen",
            markdown: r#"
Five sites cover the valves (Felner, ch. 22; Alpert, ch. 26):

* **Aortic** - 2nd intercostal space, right sternal border: aortic stenosis.
* **Pulmonic** - 2nd intercostal space, left sternal border: the S2 split, pulmonary stenosis.
* **Erb's point** - 3rd intercostal space, left sternal border: aortic regurgitation.
* **Tricuspid** - lower left sternal border: tricuspid regurgitation, ventricular septal defect.
* **Mitral** - the apex: mitral regurgitation and stenosis, S3 and S4.

Aortic stenosis is loaded and the stethoscope is at the **apex**. Open the **Display** popup, under **Listening site**,
and move it to the aortic area: the murmur grows. The **Auscultation Quiz** (Practise) asks you to
do the same with a hidden case, features first, diagnosis second; practise it often, since
repetition is what works (Barrett et al. 2004).
"#,
            actions: [
                Ui(LoadScenario("aortic_stenosis_severe")),
                Ui(FocusPanel(Simulator)),
                Ui(HeartSoundAudio(true)),
                Ui(HeartSoundLane(true)),
                Ui(Listen(site: Mitral, chest: Diaphragm)),
                Ui(SetSpeed(1.0)),
            ],
            check: Some(Question(
                prompt: "Where is the murmur of aortic stenosis loudest?",
                choices: [
                    "2nd intercostal space, right sternal border",
                    "The apex",
                    "Lower left sternal border",
                    "2nd intercostal space, left sternal border",
                ],
                answer: 0,
                explanation: "The aortic area, at the 2nd right intercostal space, lies over the outflow the jet passes through.",
            )),
        ),
    ],
)
